{"name":"Frontier Brown","hex":"#9a8172","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"frontier","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The part of a country which borders or faces another country or unsettled region.","The most advanced or recent version of something; the leading edge."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To live as pioneers on frontier territory.","To place on the frontier."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/frontier"},{"term":"brown","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A colour like that of chocolate or coffee.","One of the colour balls used in snooker, with a value of 4 points."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a brown color/colour.","Gloomy."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/brown"}],"entries":[{"title":"Frontier","blocks":[{"text":"A frontier is a political and geographical term referring to areas near or beyond a boundary, particularly zones at the edge of settled or governed territory. The concept carries distinct meanings in different national and cultural contexts, but broadly describes a transitional zone between an established civilization and unknown, ungoverned, or contested land. In colonial and post-colonial history, frontiers played a central role in the expansion of European powers into the Americas, Asia, and Australia. Countries such as Brazil, Argentina, Chile, and the United States each developed distinct frontier cultures, mythologies, and policies shaped by encounters with indigenous peoples, geographic conditions, and imperialist ambitions. In the United States, the concept was most influentially theorized by historian Frederick Jackson Turner, whose Frontier Thesis (1893) argued that the frontier experience was the defining force in shaping American democracy and national character. Canada developed a parallel historiographical tradition through scholars such as Harold Adams Innis. Beyond the Americas, frontier zones shaped imperial expansion in Russia, China, and the Indian subcontinent. The word \"frontier\" entered English from French in the 15th century with the meaning \"borderland\"; its use to describe the edge of settled territory is a distinctly North American development."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/1800s_Texas_House.JPG/330px-1800s_Texas_House.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frontier","wikidata":"Q1355821"},{"title":"Brown","blocks":[{"text":"Brown is a color. It can be thought as a darker, typically desaturated shade of orange, and can often be produced by combining red and yellow with another color, namely blue."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Espresso-roasted_coffee_beans.jpg/330px-Espresso-roasted_coffee_beans.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brown","wikidata":"Q47071"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/StateLibQld_1_113072_Bushman_with_his_dog_and_horse_outside_a_humpy%2C_Hughenden_district%5E%2C_1910-1920.jpg/500px-StateLibQld_1_113072_Bushman_with_his_dog_and_horse_outside_a_humpy%2C_Hughenden_district%5E%2C_1910-1920.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/StateLibQld_1_113072_Bushman_with_his_dog_and_horse_outside_a_humpy%2C_Hughenden_district%5E%2C_1910-1920.jpg/500px-StateLibQld_1_113072_Bushman_with_his_dog_and_horse_outside_a_humpy%2C_Hughenden_district%5E%2C_1910-1920.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Australian bushman with his dog and horse, c. 1910","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStateLibQld_1_113072_Bushman_with_his_dog_and_horse_outside_a_humpy%2C_Hughenden_district%5E%2C_1910-1920.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9